.TH SHOOK 1 "2026" "shook 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shook \- run a chain of commands in order, and stop at the first that fails
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shook run
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-stdin
.BR none | replay | pass ]
.I chain
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B shook version
.SH DESCRIPTION
The base a front runs its chains on. A front, such as
.BR git\-shook (1)
for git's hooks, decides what a chain holds, when it runs and what its output
means; shook runs it, and does nothing else.
.PP
A chain is a directory, and one that is absent or empty passes. Each regular
file in it is an entry, run one at a time in the byte order of the names. An
entry's first line is the directory it runs in, relative to
.I \-\-root
(the current directory by default), empty for the root itself, and every line
after it is one argument, the first the program. An entry with no argument is
skipped. The arguments after the chain are appended to every entry's own.
.PP
Stdin is
.B none
by default, every entry reading
.IR /dev/null ;
.B replay
reads it once and gives every entry its own copy;
.B pass
lets the entries inherit it as it is. Stdout and stderr are the entries' own.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every entry passed, or there was none.
.TP
.B 64
A mistake in the call.
.TP
.B 127
An entry's directory could not be entered; the chain stops there.
.TP
.I other
The status of the first entry that failed; the entries after it did not run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-shook (1)
.SH AUTHOR
orgrinrt <orgrinrt@ikiuni.dev>
